1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,660
you're paying for AI capability.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,780
But the real skill is knowing when to use

3
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:06,020
which level of capability.

4
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:08,240
Claude is not just one model.

5
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:09,720
It gives you a set of models

6
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:11,460
and each one has a different job.

7
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:13,140
Let's look at the model lineup.

8
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:14,200
Start with Sonnet.

9
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:16,660
Claude Sonnet 5 is now the default model

10
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:17,700
for free and pro users.

11
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,300
It is also the model most people should use

12
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:21,260
most of the time.

13
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:22,980
Anthropic describes it

14
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,240
as the most agentic Sonnet model yet.

15
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:27,220
That means it is better at planning,

16
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:28,380
using tools,

17
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:30,880
following through on multi-step tasks

18
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,040
and handling everyday knowledge work.

19
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,060
Think drafting, summarizing,

20
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:36,120
research synthesis,

21
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:37,380
artifact review,

22
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:39,720
coding and workflow automation.

23
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:41,940
For most leadership tasks,

24
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:43,960
Sonnet is the right starting point.

25
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:46,320
It gives you a strong balance of speed,

26
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:47,920
quality and cost.

27
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,520
Then there is Haiku, the lightweight model.

28
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:54,020
Use Haiku when speed matters more than depth.

29
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:55,820
It is useful for quick summaries,

30
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:58,400
simple rewrites, classification, routing

31
00:00:58,400 --> 00:00:59,860
or high volume tasks

32
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:01,700
where you do not need deep reasoning.

33
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:03,900
Then there is Opus.

34
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,760
Opus 5 is the deeper reasoning model

35
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:09,000
built for complex enterprise work

36
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,020
and long horizon agentic tasks.

37
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:12,820
As Anthropic stated,

38
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,380
it is particularly strong

39
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:16,640
at checking and correcting its own work.

40
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:18,520
Use it when the work is harder,

41
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:20,340
messier or higher stakes.

42
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,720
For example, a strategic decision,

43
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,600
a complex financial analysis,

44
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:27,560
a sensitive executive message

45
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,440
or a multi-step task

46
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:30,500
where the first answer

47
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,400
needs to be more complete and considered.

48
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:35,760
Think of it as the specialist you bring in

49
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,420
when Sonnet's first pass is not enough.

50
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:40,740
Start with high effort

51
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:44,000
and only move higher for genuinely demanding work.

52
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:47,520
Lower effort can still produce strong results

53
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:49,040
with less time and usage.

54
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:52,560
Fable 5.1 is Anthropic's most powerful

55
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:54,060
publicly available model.

56
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:56,620
It is built for hard thinking tasks

57
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:58,740
and for AI systems that can plan

58
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:01,020
and carry out many steps over time.

59
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:03,480
For example, it can research a topic,

60
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:05,000
create a plan, write documents,

61
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:06,700
improve them based on feedback

62
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:08,760
and manage tasks across tools

63
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:10,400
with little human help.

64
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:13,100
The latest version also cut down

65
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:15,020
on unnecessary safety refusals

66
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:16,600
on everyday technical questions

67
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:19,560
and made long context work cheaper to run.

68
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,160
However, as the most capable model,

69
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:24,900
Fable 5.1 will draw down your usage

70
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:26,840
much faster than the other models

71
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,020
so it is best suited for teams

72
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:30,880
building serious AI systems

73
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,420
rather than most daily individual tasks.

74
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:37,020
Now, where do you find and switch these models?

75
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:39,340
To switch models, start a new chat

76
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:40,680
and click the model name

77
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,640
in the right corner of the prompt box.

78
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:44,500
That opens the model picker

79
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:46,400
where you'll see your available options.

80
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:48,940
You'll also notice the effort options

81
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:50,520
from the model selection dropdown.

82
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:53,640
This lets you decide how much effort Claude

83
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:54,780
should put into a response.

84
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,160
Keep in mind, the higher the effort,

85
00:02:57,280 --> 00:02:58,140
the slower the response

86
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,700
and the faster the credits run out.

87
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,020
So how should you choose?

88
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:03,560
Use haiku when the task is simple

89
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:04,460
and speed matters.

90
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:07,160
Use sonnet for most daily work,

91
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,120
drafting, planning, summarizing, analysis,

92
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:12,120
coding and working with documents.

93
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:14,740
Use opus when the task needs

94
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:17,040
deeper judgment or a stronger first pass.

95
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:19,520
Use Fable when you are building

96
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:22,160
with the API, working with very long context

97
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:25,220
or designing a long running agentic workflow.

98
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:29,300
Sonnet 5 is available across all plans

99
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,220
and is the default for free in Pro.

100
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,440
Opus 5 is available on paid plans

101
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:36,600
with usage limits depending on the plan.

102
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:39,680
Because it uses more compute than sonnet,

103
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:41,580
it will draw down your allowance faster,

104
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:43,940
especially at higher effort settings.

105
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:47,380
Claude Fable 5 is also not on the free tier

106
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:49,240
and with capped usage at a maximum

107
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:52,760
of 50% of a user's weekly subscription limit,

108
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:54,940
depending on your specific account tier.

109
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:57,060
So keep in mind, the model you pick

110
00:03:57,060 --> 00:03:59,320
shapes the result before you write a single word.

111
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:01,140
Start making that distinction today

112
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:03,140
and you'll get more out of Claude.
